{尊敬語[そんけいご]} (respectful language) is used for the actions of someone **above you or outside your group** — a customer, a client, your boss, a teacher. It raises them up.

The most common verbs have special replacement forms that you simply have to memorize:
- {いる} / {行[い]く} / {来[く]る} → {いらっしゃる}
- {言[い]う} → {おっしゃる}
- {食[た]べる} / {飲[の]む} → {召[め]し上[あ]がる}
- {見[み]る} → {ご覧[らん]になる}
- {する} → {なさる}
- {くれる} → {くださる}
- {知[し]っている} → {ご存[ぞん]じだ}

**Watch the {ます}-form:** {いらっしゃる}, {おっしゃる}, {なさる}, {くださる} become {いらっしゃいます}, {おっしゃいます}, {なさいます}, {くださいます} — with {い}, not {り}.

Never use {尊敬語[そんけいご]} for your own actions.

For verbs without a special honorific form, you can build one with the pattern {お + stem + になる}.

{帰[かえ]る} → {お帰[かえ]りになる}
{書[か]く} → {お書[か]きになる}
{待[ま]つ} → {お待[ま]ちになる}

For {する}-nouns, mostly words of Chinese origin, use {ご}: {利用[りよう]する} → {ご利用[りよう]になる}, {出席[しゅっせき]する} → {ご出席[しゅっせき]になる}.

This is a high level of respect, very common in business when talking about the actions of clients, executives and customers.

**Don't use it for verbs that have special forms:** say {ご覧[らん]になる} (not {お見[み]になる}), {いらっしゃる} (not {お行[い]きになる}). Many verbs with a one-syllable stem, like {見[み]る}, {寝[ね]る}, {いる}, don't take this pattern.

{謙譲語[けんじょうご]} (humble language) is used for **your own actions** (and those of your group) in order to show respect to the listener by lowering yourself.

The key special verbs:
- {行[い]く} / {来[く]る} → {参[まい]る}; visiting someone → {伺[うかが]う}
- {いる} → {おる}
- {言[い]う} → {申[もう]す} / {申[もう]し上[あ]げる}
- {する} → {いたす}
- {食[た]べる} / {飲[の]む} / {もらう} → {いただく}
- {見[み]る} → {拝見[はいけん]する}
- {知[し]っている} → {存[ぞん]じている}
- {会[あ]う} → {お目[め]にかかる}

**Very important in Japanese companies:** when speaking to someone **outside** your company, your own boss counts as part of your group. So you use humble forms for your boss and drop {さん}: {田中[たなか]はただいま席[せき]を外[はず]しております。} 'Tanaka is away from his desk at the moment.'

The general humble pattern is {お + stem + する}. Use it for **your own** action when it affects or is done for the respected person.

{持[も]つ} → {お持[も]ちする}: {お持[も]ちしましょうか。} 'Shall I carry that for you?'
{送[おく]る} → {お送[おく]りする}
{連絡[れんらく]する} → {ご連絡[れんらく]する}
{案内[あんない]する} → {ご案内[あんない]する}

Replacing {する} with {いたす} makes it even more humble: {ご連絡[れんらく]いたします}. This is standard in business emails.

**Two common mistakes:**
- The action must involve the other person. You can't say {×お食[た]べする} for eating your own lunch.
- Never use it for the other person's actions. {×お客様[きゃくさま]がお待[ま]ちしています} is wrong; say {お待[ま]ちになっています} (honorific).

{丁寧語[ていねいご]} (polite language) makes your speech more polite **without** raising or lowering anyone. Beyond the basic {です} / {ます}, there's a top tier used by shop staff, hotel staff, and in formal business:

- {あります} → {ございます}: {ご質問[しつもん]はございますか。}
- {です} → {でございます}: {お手洗[てあら]いはあちらでございます。}
- {いいですか} → {よろしいでしょうか}
- {ちょっと} → {少々[しょうしょう]}; {今日[きょう]} → {本日[ほんじつ]}; {こっち} → {こちら}

**Careful:** {でございます} is for things and for yourself. When asking about the other **person**, use the honorific {でいらっしゃいます}: {山田[やまだ]様[さま]でいらっしゃいますか。} 'Are you Mr. Yamada?' Saying {山田[やまだ]様[さま]でございますか} is heard sometimes, but it's considered incorrect.

{〜させていただく} literally means 'to humbly receive (your) letting me do ~'. It presents your own action as something you do **with the listener's permission or thanks to them**. It is one of the most common phrases in Japanese business.

{では、会議[かいぎ]を始[はじ]めさせていただきます。} 'Well then, allow me to begin the meeting.'
{体調[たいちょう]が悪[わる]いので、早[はや]めに帰[かえ]らせていただきます。} 'I'm not feeling well, so I'll leave early, if you'll allow me.'

**Form:** causative {て}-form + {いただく}.
- Group 1: {帰[かえ]る} → {帰[かえ]らせて}, {読[よ]む} → {読[よ]ませて}
- Group 2: {始[はじ]める} → {始[はじ]めさせて}
- {する} → {させて}; {来[く]る} → {来[こ]させて}

A common error is adding an extra {さ} to group 1 verbs: {×読[よ]まさせていただく} → {読[よ]ませていただく}. Some people also overuse this phrase where no permission is involved, which can sound stiff.
